@page rh="EXODUS · 15 OF 17 · WHAT WAS DONE WITH IT" m="ISA 43.16–21; 1 COR 10.1–5; RABOTEAU, SLAVE RELIGION (1978); CONE, A BLACK THEOLOGY OF LIBERATION (1970); WARRIOR, “CANAANITES, COWBOYS, AND INDIANS” (1989); TKC: DOM · TL"
# The most borrowed book
Exodus is quoted by the enslaved and the enslavers, by prophets, empires, movements and settlers. For a novelist writing an empire with a scripture, the reception history is primary material.
rec:
Inside the Bible | The prophets judge Israel by the deliverance, the return from exile is told in exodus language, the gospels put Jesus in a wilderness and on a mountain, and Paul reads the crossing as a figure (1 Cor 10). Reuse starts inside the canon.
The spirituals | Enslaved Black Americans made Exodus the center of a sung theology, Pharaoh, the river, the promised land, often with a double meaning, a coded present inside a biblical past. "Go Down, Moses" was among the first spirituals published, in 1861, and Harriet Tubman was called Moses. For this companion, the most important reception stream.
Slaveholder use | The same book was preached to the enslaved with the deliverance removed and the obedience kept, and the covenant code and Leviticus 25 were cited to show scripture regulates slavery and so allows it. The text didn't prevent this. The selection did it.
The founders | In 1776 Benjamin Franklin proposed a Great Seal showing Pharaoh drowning, under the motto "Rebellion to tyrants is obedience to God."
Liberation theology | James Cone made the God of the exodus the center of *A Black Theology of Liberation* (1970) and *God of the Oppressed* (1975). God is disclosed as one who takes sides.
The Canaanite problem | Robert Allen Warrior read the story from the Canaanites' side in 1989. The exodus ends in someone else's land, and for Native Americans the Puritans had played Israel. A companion that celebrates the exodus without naming this hasn't finished reading.
The civil rights era | Movement preaching ran on Pharaoh, the wilderness and the mountaintop. Martin Luther King Jr.'s last sermon, in Memphis on April 3, 1968, ended with Moses' view of the promised land: "I may not get there with you."
prompt: Which reception stream my characters were raised inside, and who edited it for them | fill

@page rh="EXODUS · 16 OF 17 · DOMINION'S EXODUS" m="EXOD 20.2; EXOD 20.8–11; EXOD 22.25–27; TKC: DOM · SANCTUM; FR 33"
# A deliverance already spent
POSSIBLE: An empire can't suppress this book, because it's too well known. So it dates it. The deliverance is taught as accomplished, in the founding, by the Empire itself, and what's left for the present is the obedience section. Everything below is a TKC construction to test against the manuscript.
tabrl:
| What the Sanctum circulates | What it keeps in the closed catechism |
| The sea. Deliverance as a founding event, already granted. | Chapter 1. The resistance began with two working women who lied to a king and were rewarded. |
| The commandments, from the second clause on. | The preamble. The authority to command rests on having ended somebody's forced labor. |
| Sabbath as household order and public observance. | Sabbath as a limit on what an owner may take from a worker, an animal or a foreigner. |
| The tabernacle chapters. Costly, ordered, beautiful worship. | The seventh-year release, the interest ban and the returned cloak. |
| Moses the lawgiver, obedient and unquestioning. | Moses arguing God out of a decision, twice, and offering to be struck from the book. |
box: The Sanctum's problem with this book
POSSIBLE: A scripture whose God is introduced as having heard a labor complaint can't be fully tamed. Someone in the Temple has to manage that. Whether it sits with the Prelate's office is open. The Prelate's working name is Aurelia (POSSIBLE, FR 33).
/end
corr: SUPERSEDED: The old companion marked "Prelate Huldah's office" as canon and gave it the Empire's handling of Exodus. FR 33 renamed the Prelate, and the office's role here is only POSSIBLE now.
prompt: The Empire's deliverance liturgy. Write the part a character stops believing | fill

